From 610676118e041f14fe49debfd8d8fdf68593d990 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:52:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-backend: do not compute a split with a custom op behind
 a host gate

A late CPU split runs while the GPU stream waits on the host gate. The
gate opens only after the split is done, so nothing in the split may wait
for that stream. ggml_cuda_graph_ready checks this for the scheduler's own
launch, but not for the work of the CPU split.

A custom op can call into any backend. The MoE split (LLAMA_MOE_SPLIT)
does: begin_op registers host memory (cudaHostRegister) and creates the
helper's backend on its first call, and the helper thread can grow its
graph buffer (cudaFree), launch kernels for the first time (lazy module
load) and instantiate CUDA graphs. Each of these can wait for the whole
device, which includes the gated stream, while the scheduler thread spins
in end_op: a deadlock. The gated GPU split is only needed to be a warm
CUDA graph, so a prefill ubatch of a size seen twice in 5 s, whose earlier
runs had no expert above the threshold, reaches this.

The scheduler now does not compute a split late if it has a custom op.
Only prefill ubatches with the split lose the gate (a few us per layer
against ms of CPU work); decode and verify graphs keep it. Exact: the
order of work changes, not the work.

test-backend-sched-gate: a fake GPU backend with a host gate, on the CPU.
A plain CPU op is gated; a custom op must not run behind a closed gate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01C6MCJC4HTKxvuGjKjqDuqQ
